WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:06.120
Why would a cell make a protein all day, only to destroy it?

2
00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:10.720
Most of your cells do exactly that with free beta β-catenin.

3
00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:15.760
It looks like waste, yet it is a fast, responsive control switch.

4
00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:19.180
Start with a cell receiving no signal.

5
00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:24.320
Stable beta β-catenin sits locked at cell junctions, untouched here.

6
00:00:25.580 --> 00:00:28.220
Every free copy meets the destruction complex.

7
00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:34.180
A violet scaffold called Axin holds two kinases beside beta β-catenin.

8
00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:39.560
A large partner called APC helps the complex finish its job.

9
00:00:40.633 --> 00:00:44.833
First, CK1 adds a priming phosphate to beta β-catenin.

10
00:00:45.893 --> 00:00:49.653
That lets GSK3 add three more phosphates in sequence.

11
00:00:51.133 --> 00:00:57.093
Next, β-TrCP grips the tagged protein inside an SCF ubiquitin ligase.

12
00:00:57.093 --> 00:00:59.913
The proteasome then shreds it.

13
00:01:00.867 --> 00:01:05.507
Now picture the steady level of free beta β-catenin like water in a tank.

14
00:01:06.747 --> 00:01:10.547
A running tap sets synthesis, while an open drain sets destruction.

15
00:01:11.947 --> 00:01:16.967
If a signal slows destruction to one quarter while synthesis stays steady, how does

16
00:01:16.967 --> 00:01:17.967
the level change?

17
00:01:23.833 --> 00:01:25.513
The level rises fourfold.

18
00:01:26.733 --> 00:01:31.233
At steady state, level equals synthesis divided by the destruction rate.

19
00:01:32.573 --> 00:01:37.333
12 over 0.4 gives 30, but 12 over 0.1 gives 120.

20
00:01:38.733 --> 00:01:40.033
Synthesis never changed.

21
00:01:41.153 --> 00:01:47.193
That is how the signal we meet next, Wnt, raises free beta β-catenin: by slowing its

22
00:01:47.193 --> 00:01:48.213
constant destruction.

23
00:01:49.367 --> 00:01:53.967
That signal is Wnt, a secreted protein carrying a fatty acid tail.

24
00:01:55.267 --> 00:02:00.307
Outside the cell, Wnt grips a teal receptor called Frizzled using that lipid tail.

25
00:02:01.587 --> 00:02:07.027
At the same time, Wnt brings Frizzled together with a slate-blue partner, LRP5 or

26
00:02:07.027 --> 00:02:08.027
LRP6.

27
00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:13.060
Inside the cell, Frizzled recruits an amber protein called Dishevelled.

28
00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:19.740
Meanwhile, the inner tail of LRP6 gains phosphates and grabs the Axin scaffold.

29
00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:25.620
The destruction complex moves up to the receptor, where it stops delivering beta

30
00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:27.860
β-catenin efficiently to the ligase.

31
00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:34.020
How the receptor disables the complex is still debated, yet the result is clear.

32
00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:40.540
Newly made coral beta β-catenin survives, fills the cytoplasm, and enters the

33
00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:47.060
nucleus. There it finds emerald TCF on target DNA, where grey Groucho kept genes

34
00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:48.060
off.

35
00:02:48.383 --> 00:02:53.863
In the simple model, beta β-catenin displaces Groucho and brings in proteins that

36
00:02:53.863 --> 00:02:55.223
switch target genes on.

37
00:02:56.463 --> 00:03:01.763
Notice what happens if a mutation breaks APC or the phosphate sites on beta

38
00:03:01.763 --> 00:03:07.903
β-catenin. Destruction slows even with no Wnt, leaving those target genes stuck on.

39
00:03:08.917 --> 00:03:13.397
Control over free beta β-catenin rests on destruction, not synthesis.

40
00:03:14.797 --> 00:03:17.497
Keep the drain open and target genes stay silent.

41
00:03:18.857 --> 00:03:23.077
Slow the drain with Wnt, and free beta β-catenin climbs to switch genes on.

42
00:03:24.317 --> 00:03:27.657
The cell turns the pathway on by slowing constant destruction.
